6. PHASE FIVE — ROLE-BASED ACCESS CONTROL

Use explicit, server-enforced authorization with least privilege and deny-by-default behavior.

Implement the following baseline roles.

6.1 Administrator

May manage institutional records, academic structures, users, assignments, publication workflows, and reports only within explicitly granted administrative permissions.

Sensitive operations must be authorized and audited. Avoid granting every account unrestricted administrative privileges.

Where useful, separate routine administration from security-sensitive account and role management.

6.2 Teacher or staff member

May access only the information required by their role and authorized institutional assignments.

Teaching-related permissions may include:

- Viewing assigned classes and relevant enrolled students.
- Recording attendance for authorized sessions.
- Accessing authorized timetable information.
- Creating or updating permitted announcements.
- Reviewing reports within the assigned scope.

Do not allow a teacher to access all student records merely because they are a teacher.

General staff permissions must be defined separately from teaching permissions where their duties differ.

6.3 Student

May access only their own authorized information, such as:

- Their own profile information.
- Their own enrolments and timetable.
- Their own attendance information.
- Published announcements.
- Other explicitly authorized self-service information.

Students must not be able to access or modify another student's private information.

6.4 Permission implementation

For every protected operation, document:

- Required role or permission.
- Permitted data scope.
- Server-side enforcement point.
- Database-level enforcement where supported.
- Audit requirement.
- Positive test.
- Negative or adversarial test.

Do not rely on hidden navigation items, client-side checks, unguessable identifiers, or frontend role checks as security controls.

Scope queries to the requesting user's authorized records. Test direct requests, altered record identifiers, unauthorized role changes, and attempts to bypass the interface.

Additional roles, including parents or guardians, must not be introduced without an explicit requirements and security assessment.

