12. PHASE ELEVEN — IMPLEMENTATION PROTOCOL

Work in verified increments, preserving a coherent and usable application throughout development.

Phase 1: Environment and baseline

Inspect the workspace, runtime, tools, existing files, integrations, and deployment options.

Deliver an environment report and identify the real execution boundaries.

Phase 2: Research and specification

Investigate official institutional sources, Humans of CEK, other relevant social media sources, comparable institutions, and appropriate technical references.

Create the source inventory, evidence ledger, requirements backlog, sitemap, permission matrix, initial content model, design direction, and architecture decisions.

Do not delay independent technical foundation work while research continues.

Phase 3: Foundation

Establish the application structure, configuration, dependencies, database schema, migrations, authentication foundation, authorization model, design tokens, shared layout, and reusable components.

Use synthetic seed data.

Phase 4: Public website

Implement the research-informed public routes, navigation, verified content, accessible responsive layouts, editorial storytelling, notices, and admissions discovery.

Use explicit placeholders for unresolved institutional facts.

Phase 5: Core IMS

Implement authentication, account management, role-aware navigation, student and staff records, academic relationships, enrolments, teaching assignments, attendance, timetables, and announcements in dependency order.

Prioritize complete workflows over superficial breadth.

Phase 6: Editorial operations

Integrate source attribution, content verification, media permissions, review and approval, publication, and archival where justified by the MVP requirements.

Implement the social media strategy and editorial templates as actionable deliverables.

Phase 7: Integration

Connect interfaces to real application logic and persistent storage.

Remove placeholder interactions from workflows represented as complete.

Implement validation, authorization, loading states, mutation feedback, error recovery, and data consistency.

Phase 8: Verification

Run relevant formatting checks, type checks, linting, unit tests, integration tests, end-to-end tests, migrations, and production builds.

Fix discovered defects rather than suppressing failures or weakening tests.

Phase 9: Visual and functional refinement

Inspect actual rendered pages where possible. Test responsive layouts, navigation, forms, permissions, content publishing, keyboard access, and accessibility.

Implement a second refinement pass based on observed defects.

Phase 10: Delivery and handover

Provide the verified implementation, setup instructions, configuration documentation, migrations, test results, deployment guidance, known limitations, and prioritized remaining backlog.

If credentials, infrastructure, or permissions prevent deployment, provide a reproducible deployment procedure instead of claiming a live release.

